  "description": "<p>ARCH (Adaptive Reactive CUDA Hydrodynamics) is a C++20 and CUDA simulation code for finite-volume compressible hydrodynamics, heat transfer and nuclear reactions with adaptive mesh refinement. CPU and CUDA backends retain shared physical and numerical formulations. The optional ARCH Studio provides parameter editing, configuration and builds, initial-field and AMR previews, run and restart control, and read-only plotfile inspection.</p><p>Version 1.3.1 is a maintenance source release. It updates the Studio development dependency Vite from 8.3.0 to 8.3.4 with its locked transitive dependencies, removes machine-local assistant instructions and completed execution plans from distribution, and refreshes release and citation documentation. Scientific implementation, mathematical libraries, Studio data-reading and data-writing implementation, scientific inputs, test definitions, tolerances and historical acceptance evidence are unchanged by this maintenance release.</p><p>Supported execution remains Linux and WSL2 under the qualification recorded for version 1.3.0. Windows native execution, MPI, additional GPU vendors and additional platforms are planning topics and are not qualified by this release. Existing limitations, including retained historical failures and gaps, remain applicable. No new scientific validation or general performance claim follows from the dependency update.</p><p>The archive contains tagged source, third-party notices and existing validation evidence. No raw simulation outputs, prebuilt scientific binaries or fetched Git LFS payloads are added. Git LFS pointers remain pointers; obtain required external data and system dependencies using the build guide. All ARCH-authored material is MIT licensed; third-party material retains its own terms.</p><p>AI-assisted tools were used for bug tracking and fixes, review of tolerance settings across the complete CTest suite, selected documentation, code completion, and engineering and security checks. Human contributors designed the overall software architecture and retain responsibility for scientific definitions, numerical acceptance, code review and release decisions.</p>",
  "keywords": [
